WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.566 --> 00:00:02.480
(dramatic instrumental music)

00:00:02.480 --> 00:00:05.580
- [Narrator] This is the
engine of a Boeing 777

00:00:05.580 --> 00:00:07.863
that failed on February 20th, 2021.

00:00:09.500 --> 00:00:12.110
- [Pilot] United 328 heavy
mayday, mayday aircraft.

00:00:12.110 --> 00:00:14.640
- It was flying from Denver to Honolulu

00:00:14.640 --> 00:00:16.873
with 241 people on board.

00:00:17.990 --> 00:00:19.250
Parts of it rained down

00:00:19.250 --> 00:00:21.733
on a residential neighborhood
near the airport.

00:00:22.620 --> 00:00:24.636
Pilots were able to land the plane safely.

00:00:24.636 --> 00:00:26.290
- [Passenger] Yay!
(passengers cheering)

00:00:26.290 --> 00:00:28.020
- [Narrator] And no one was injured.

00:00:28.020 --> 00:00:30.170
But it wasn't the first
time this happened.

00:00:31.100 --> 00:00:36.100
The same type of engine, the
Pratt &amp; Whitney PW4000-112,

00:00:36.240 --> 00:00:40.070
also failed on a Japan Airlines
flight in December 2020

00:00:40.070 --> 00:00:42.270
and another failed on a
United Airlines flight

00:00:42.270 --> 00:00:43.963
in February 2018.

00:00:44.900 --> 00:00:48.140
In each case, regulators found
that the engine fan blades

00:00:48.140 --> 00:00:50.720
fractured due to fatigue or cracks.

00:00:50.720 --> 00:00:52.400
In the 2018 incident,

00:00:52.400 --> 00:00:55.710
the National Transportation
Safety Board, or NTSB,

00:00:55.710 --> 00:00:57.340
found that flawed inspections

00:00:57.340 --> 00:01:01.090
by the engine maker Pratt &amp;
Whitney were the root cause.

00:01:01.090 --> 00:01:04.050
The agency is still
investigating all possible causes

00:01:04.050 --> 00:01:05.480
of the most recent failures.

00:01:05.480 --> 00:01:06.917
- [Controller] Denver-departure of United-

00:01:06.917 --> 00:01:10.177
- [Narrator] Including Pratt &amp;
Whitney's inspection process.

00:01:10.177 --> 00:01:14.720
(dramatic instrumental music)

00:01:14.720 --> 00:01:18.780
The 1990s brought a lot of
changes to commercial aviation.

00:01:18.780 --> 00:01:21.630
New planes, like the
Boeing 777 were introduced,

00:01:21.630 --> 00:01:23.740
along with new engines to power them,

00:01:23.740 --> 00:01:26.333
like the Pratt &amp; Whitney PW4000.

00:01:27.250 --> 00:01:29.660
Modern engines like the PW4000

00:01:29.660 --> 00:01:32.500
feature lighter and
more aerodynamic blades.

00:01:32.500 --> 00:01:34.460
Instead of a solid piece of metal,

00:01:34.460 --> 00:01:37.740
some fan blades have a
hollow honeycomb interior.

00:01:37.740 --> 00:01:39.090
But this style of fan blade

00:01:39.090 --> 00:01:42.970
required new methods of
inspecting them for wear and tear.

00:01:42.970 --> 00:01:47.190
In 2005, Pratt &amp; Whitney
developed a new inspection method

00:01:47.190 --> 00:01:48.830
using an existing technology

00:01:48.830 --> 00:01:51.290
called thermal acoustic imaging.

00:01:51.290 --> 00:01:53.250
The fan blades are hit with sound waves

00:01:53.250 --> 00:01:56.020
that cause the structure
to subtly vibrate.

00:01:56.020 --> 00:01:57.330
If there's a crack somewhere

00:01:57.330 --> 00:01:59.340
in the interior of the fan blade,

00:01:59.340 --> 00:02:02.710
the vibration will cause
friction and therefore heat

00:02:02.710 --> 00:02:04.310
which an inspector's instruments

00:02:04.310 --> 00:02:06.603
can detect on the fan blade's surface.

00:02:10.500 --> 00:02:14.050
- [Announcer] The PW4000 has
a state of the art design.

00:02:14.050 --> 00:02:16.890
The result of more than
10 years of research

00:02:16.890 --> 00:02:18.873
and hundreds of tests.

00:02:21.310 --> 00:02:22.630
- [Narrator] In the 1990s,

00:02:22.630 --> 00:02:24.990
the aerospace industry
took a more active role

00:02:24.990 --> 00:02:27.430
in setting training and
certification requirements

00:02:27.430 --> 00:02:29.400
for aircraft inspections.

00:02:29.400 --> 00:02:31.220
The FAA maintained oversight,

00:02:31.220 --> 00:02:34.333
but the industry had more
input in developing standards.

00:02:35.210 --> 00:02:37.490
The aerospace industry maintains standards

00:02:37.490 --> 00:02:39.890
for a list of established
inspection methods.

00:02:39.890 --> 00:02:41.460
Inspectors are required to go through

00:02:41.460 --> 00:02:43.040
hundreds of hours of training

00:02:43.040 --> 00:02:46.170
to obtain certifications
to use these methods.

00:02:46.170 --> 00:02:48.850
But when an aerospace
company, like Pratt &amp; Whitney,

00:02:48.850 --> 00:02:50.850
introduces a new inspection technique,

00:02:50.850 --> 00:02:52.650
like thermal acoustic imagining,

00:02:52.650 --> 00:02:55.670
it's classified as an emerging technology.

00:02:55.670 --> 00:02:58.140
This classification allows manufacturers,

00:02:58.140 --> 00:03:01.050
like Pratt &amp; Whitney, to start
using a new inspection method

00:03:01.050 --> 00:03:04.180
without first developing
a training program.

00:03:04.180 --> 00:03:06.150
With oversight from the FAA,

00:03:06.150 --> 00:03:08.220
the manufacturer is supposed to formalize

00:03:08.220 --> 00:03:10.760
the inspection method over time.

00:03:10.760 --> 00:03:14.150
But the NTSB found that because
thermal acoustic imagining

00:03:14.150 --> 00:03:16.960
was still classified as
an emerging technology,

00:03:16.960 --> 00:03:20.130
Pratt &amp; Whitney never developed
a formal training program,

00:03:20.130 --> 00:03:21.760
even thought they'd used it to inspect

00:03:21.760 --> 00:03:25.313
more than 9,000 fan blades
over the course of 13 years.

00:03:27.560 --> 00:03:29.390
Pratt &amp; Whitney declined to comment,

00:03:29.390 --> 00:03:31.820
citing ongoing investigations.

00:03:31.820 --> 00:03:33.460
But the company previously said

00:03:33.460 --> 00:03:36.730
that it would work with
regulators on new inspections.

00:03:36.730 --> 00:03:40.520
An FAA spokesman said the
issue with the 2018 failure

00:03:40.520 --> 00:03:43.190
was with the interpretation
of the inspection result,

00:03:43.190 --> 00:03:45.770
not the execution and that Pratt &amp; Whitney

00:03:45.770 --> 00:03:47.453
has since corrected the errors.

00:03:52.310 --> 00:03:55.710
When the engine that failed
in 2018 was last inspected,

00:03:55.710 --> 00:03:58.220
the P&amp;W employees that inspected it

00:03:58.220 --> 00:04:00.180
did not attend one of
the training sessions

00:04:00.180 --> 00:04:02.170
on how to use the technology.

00:04:02.170 --> 00:04:03.490
This was only possible

00:04:03.490 --> 00:04:06.283
because it was classified
as an emerging technology.

00:04:07.520 --> 00:04:11.350
After the NTSB's report on
the 2018 engine failure,

00:04:11.350 --> 00:04:13.570
Pratt &amp; Whitney agreed
to formalize training

00:04:13.570 --> 00:04:15.567
for thermal acoustic imagining.

00:04:16.930 --> 00:04:20.240
The Pratt &amp; Whitney engine
that failed in February 2021

00:04:20.240 --> 00:04:22.790
was last inspected in 2016,

00:04:22.790 --> 00:04:24.500
before the new training standards,

00:04:24.500 --> 00:04:26.453
raising questions about its accuracy.

00:04:27.640 --> 00:04:30.530
Pratt &amp; Whitney reexamined the
records from that inspection

00:04:30.530 --> 00:04:32.630
and refined how it interpreted the results

00:04:32.630 --> 00:04:34.710
after the 2018 failure.

00:04:34.710 --> 00:04:37.770
The reexaminations did not
find any cracks in the blades,

00:04:37.770 --> 00:04:39.143
according to the FAA.

00:04:40.110 --> 00:04:42.010
Still, the inspection process

00:04:42.010 --> 00:04:43.640
remains one of the focal points

00:04:43.640 --> 00:04:47.410
in the NTSB's investigation
into the 2021 failure.

00:04:49.672 --> 00:04:51.230
- [Pilot] The United 238
heavy mayday aircraft

00:04:51.230 --> 00:04:53.720
just experienced an engine failure.

00:04:53.720 --> 00:04:54.970
Need to turn immediately.

00:04:56.610 --> 00:04:57.443
- [Narrator] In this video

00:04:57.443 --> 00:04:59.730
taken by a passenger
on the February flight,

00:04:59.730 --> 00:05:02.090
you can see the broken fan blade.

00:05:02.090 --> 00:05:05.573
- Regarding the fan blade that
was fractured at the root,

00:05:07.240 --> 00:05:09.960
a preliminary on-scene exam

00:05:10.860 --> 00:05:15.623
indicates damage consistent
with metal fatigue.

00:05:18.290 --> 00:05:20.090
- [Narrator] An NTSB spokesman said

00:05:20.090 --> 00:05:22.810
their investigation of
maintenance and inspection records

00:05:22.810 --> 00:05:25.740
related to the most
recent incident is ongoing

00:05:25.740 --> 00:05:29.430
and that it's too early to draw
conclusions about the cause.

00:05:29.430 --> 00:05:31.840
Boeing said it was working with regulators

00:05:31.840 --> 00:05:33.810
and that it supports the FAA's guidance

00:05:33.810 --> 00:05:35.513
on inspection requirements.

00:05:36.615 --> 00:05:37.448
- [Woman] It's so weird.

00:05:37.448 --> 00:05:38.281
There's, like, smoke coming out

00:05:38.281 --> 00:05:40.223
of one of the engines on that plane.

00:05:41.340 --> 00:05:43.430
- [Narrator] The FAA
ordered new inspections

00:05:43.430 --> 00:05:45.260
that must be completed before planes

00:05:45.260 --> 00:05:47.310
with this type of Pratt &amp; Whitney engine

00:05:47.310 --> 00:05:49.430
can be returned to service,

00:05:49.430 --> 00:05:52.848
citing the likelihood of
further fan blade failures.

00:05:52.848 --> 00:05:55.348
(tense music)

